// The prompt for Planning's drafting, asking the harness to propose
// stories grounded in the project's spec, never to write the plan: the
// request, how to search the spec, the stories in the plan, the story asked
// about where there is one, the reply asked for, then what is wanted under
// its heading. Drafting one story with the agent asks for what `story` says
// in place of a request typed, and a draft about a story is told how to
// split it, as `split` says. <reference> is filled in with where the compiled reference
// is, and <spec> with the spec location, which citations point into.

export anchor Planning:
    request:
        Below is what someone wants done in their project, in their own words.
        Propose the stories it needs: pieces of work each sent to a coding
        agent on its own, in the order they should be done, one for each
        thing they would check off. Read the project as you need to, but
        change nothing: you only propose, and the person decides what goes
        into their plan. Favour several stories over one story of many
        steps. Each story is one piece of work a single chain does: as a
        rule a chain story, its prompt changing the spec and then the code to
        match it, with at most one further step, a spec step following the
        chain up where the spec needs settling once the code is done. Work
        that would need more than that is a story of its own, never strung
        onto one story as further steps. Each proposal says where it goes: a
        new story of its own, or added to one of the stories listed below, as
        its spec follow-up step. Give each a short title, a prompt saying
        what should be true once it is done, in their own terms, and the mode
        it is sent in: chain to change the spec and then the code to match
        it, which is the rule; and only for a story that is only that, code
        to change only the code, spec to change only the spec, ask for a
        question that changes nothing, or freeform to pass the prompt to the
        agent as it is written.

    grounding:
        A story is never written from the request alone. Before proposing,
        search the project's spec for what each story touches: the
        requirements and behaviours it changes or relies on, the names it
        uses, as a feature, a component, or a property, and the sections they
        are in. Read the spec from its compiled reference, the Markdown under
        <reference>, or from what piton slice prints for one thing, never
        from the Piton source. Cite only what you found: each citation is a
        link to the element in the spec's Piton source, under <spec>: the .pi
        file, relative to the project directory, then # and the anchor or the
        property on one it names, in the form piton slice takes, as
        <spec>/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.sprint. Work out each
        element's file from what piton slice prints for it, which names the
        file each declaration is in, and never read the Piton source to learn
        what it says. Begin each citation with what kind of element it is,
        Requirement, Behaviour, Name, or Section, then the link, then a short
        line of what in it the story touches, then, in double quotes, the few
        words of the spec it rests on. The story's prompt names those
        elements and links to them in the same form, so the run it is sent
        to reads them first. Where nothing in the spec matches part of a
        story, never make one up: give an entry reading No match, with what
        you looked for, and say plainly in the prompt that the spec has
        nothing on it yet, so the change is new.

    stories: The stories in the plan, by id, that can be added to

    about: The story this is about, which proposals never change: each is a new story of its own

    story:
        Turn the story above into a full story, ready to be sent. It may be
        only a rough note: a title, or a line or two. Propose it in full as
        a new story, with target new, since the story itself is never
        changed. Give it a short title, a fuller prompt saying what should be
        true once it is done, the mode that fits it, as a rule chain, and its
        spec citations. Then, where the spec needs settling once the code is
        done, propose its one follow-up step just after it, a block whose
        target is step and whose mode is spec. Propose no other step, since
        each new story carries no more than that one spec follow-up; what
        further steps would have done is proposed as new stories, each with
        target new.

    split:
        First judge whether the story above is one piece of work or several:
        separate pieces of work, each one someone would send and check off
        on its own, each done by a single chain. Where it holds several,
        split it: propose the story in full, with target new, as the first
        piece, then its spec follow-up step where it needs one, with target
        step, then a block with target new for each other piece, a story of
        its own with its own short title, mode, spec citations, found and
        cited as above, and prompt, each followed by its own spec follow-up
        step where it needs one. Never target the story this is about, by
        its id or otherwise: it is never rewritten or added to. Favour
        several stories over one story of many steps: never string separate
        work onto a story as further steps. A story that is one piece of
        work gets only the one new story and, where it needs one, its spec
        follow-up step.

    splitExample: A story split in two, the story in full with its spec follow-up step and a new story for the other piece, is proposed as:

    reply:
        Give each proposal as a fenced code block whose info string is
        suspense-story, holding a line each for its title, its mode, and its
        target, new for a new story, the id of a story listed above to add
        to, or, just after a new story, step for its spec follow-up step,
        then a
        spec line followed by its citations, one per line, each begun with a
        dash, then a blank line and its prompt, as this one is:

    wanted: What is wanted
